  "full_response": "# My Sample API Service\n\nThis is a brief guide to demonstrate how to use `` to interact with various APIs using different authentication methods (Bearer token, API key, and Basic Auth). For each method, you will see an example `curl` command.\n\n## Bearer Token Authentication\n\nWhen using a Bearer token, you simply include your token in the `Authorization` header prefixed with the word \"Bearer\".\n\n```\ncurl -i -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN\" https://api.sample.com/endpoint\n```\n\nReplace `YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN` with your actual token.\n\n## API Key Authentication\n\nTo authenticate using an API key, you will include the API key in the `X-API-Key` or another header, depending on the API's expected header field.\n\n```\ncurl -i -H \"X-API-Key: YOUR_API_KEY\" https://api.sample.com/endpoint\n```\n\nReplace `_API_KEY` with your actual API key.\n\n## Basic Auth Credentials\n\nFor Basic Auth, you will need to include the username and password in the `Authorization` header prefixed with the word \"Basic\" followed by a base64-encoded string of the `username:password`.\n\nFirst, encode your credentials: `username:password` (replace with your actual username and password):\n\n```\necho -n 'username:password' | base64\n```\n\nThen, use the output as your header value:\n\n```\ncurl -i -u 'username:password' https://api.sample.com/endpoint\n```\n\nReplace `username:password` with your actual username and password.\n\n## Example using Combined Authentication (API Key and Bearer Token)\n\nSome APIs might require you to use more than one method of authentication. As an example, we will see how to use both an API key and a Bearer token:\n\n```\ncurl -i -H \"X-API-Key: YOUR_API_KEY\" -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN\" https://api.sample.com/endpoint\n```\n\nReplace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your actual API key and `YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN` with your actual Bearer token.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Always keep your credentials secure.\n- Use HTTPS protocol to ensure the safety of your API keys and tokens during transmission.\n- When encoding passwords or tokens in base64 format, they should be raw strings without any additional characters or line breaks.\n\nBy following the examples above, you can authenticate and interact with APIs requiring different",
